UNIT pick
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reconstruction contract for the blind re-emitter:
#   - DEFAULT EXPORT:  export default function pick(obj, keys) { ... }
#   - SELF-CONTAINED:  inline the isObject predicate (below). NO `require`,
#     NO `import`. The isobject@3.0.1 logic is reproduced verbatim in BEHAVIOR.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

SIG  pick(obj: any, keys: string | string[]) -> object
     # Returns a NEW plain object ({}) containing only those `keys` that EXIST
     # on `obj` (existence tested with the JS `in` operator). The original
     # `obj` is never mutated. The return is ALWAYS a freshly-allocated object
     # literal, even on the guard / empty paths.

DEPENDS-ON  isobject@3.0.1 (INLINED — see BEHAVIOR node B1; do not require it)

BEHAVIOR
  # Ordered pipeline, transcribed from object.pick@1.3.0/index.js.
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  # B0. isObject predicate (inlined from isobject@3.0.1):
  #     isObject(val)  ==  ( val != null
  #                          && typeof val === 'object'
  #                          && Array.isArray(val) === false )
  #     VERIFIED against the real isobject@3.0.1:
  #       isObject({})        -> true
  #       isObject([])        -> false   (arrays are EXCLUDED)
  #       isObject(null)      -> false
  #       isObject(undefined) -> false
  #       isObject(1)         -> false
  #       isObject("s")       -> false
  #       isObject(true)      -> false
  #       isObject(function(){}) -> false   (typeof function !== 'object')
  #
  # B1. GUARD (line 13):  if ( !isObject(obj) && typeof obj !== 'function' )
  #         return {};
  #     i.e. proceed only when obj is a plain non-array object OR a function.
  #     - Plain object / plain-object-like (typeof 'object', non-null,
  #       non-array)  -> PROCEED.
  #     - function    -> PROCEED (functions have own/inherited props pickable).
  #     - ARRAY       -> return {}  (isObject false, typeof 'object' not 'function').
  #     - null        -> return {}.
  #     - undefined   -> return {}.
  #     - number / string / boolean / bigint / symbol primitive -> return {}.
  #     The returned {} is a fresh empty object.
  #
  # B2. Initialize  res = {}  (fresh object literal).
  #
  # B3. BRANCH on keys type:
  #     ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #     B3a. STRING form:  if ( typeof keys === 'string' )
  #             if ( keys in obj )         # `in` operator — see NOTE-IN below
  #                 res[keys] = obj[keys];
  #             return res;                # single-key path always returns here
  #          NOTE the empty string '' is a valid string key:
  #             pick({'':9, a:1}, '')  ->  res has own key '' = 9.
  #          A string key not present (own OR inherited) is skipped -> res stays {}.
  #     ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #     B3b. ARRAY (and array-like fall-through) form:
  #             len = keys.length;  idx = -1;
  #             while ( ++idx < len ) {
  #                 key = keys[idx];
  #                 if ( key in obj )      # `in` operator
  #                     res[key] = obj[key];
  #             }
  #             return res;
  #          - Iterates positions 0..len-1 (pre-increment: first key is keys[0]).
  #          - Each present key copied; missing keys skipped.
  #          - DUPLICATE keys in the array are idempotent (same slot reassigned):
  #               pick({a:1}, ['a','a','a']) -> {a:1}.
  #          - keys[idx] is coerced to a property key when used in `in`/index
  #            (e.g. numeric array entries stringify).
  #
  # NOTE-IN (KEY-EXISTENCE OPERATOR — VERIFIED): the unit uses the JS `in`
  #   operator, NOT hasOwnProperty and NOT a truthiness check. Consequences:
  #   * `in` walks the PROTOTYPE CHAIN. So inherited keys are considered
  #     "present" and ARE copied onto res:
  #       pick({a:1}, 'toString')  copies res.toString = (inherited Object#toString).
  #       pick({a:1}, ['toString','constructor','hasOwnProperty','valueOf','a'])
  #         -> res own keys = [toString, constructor, hasOwnProperty, valueOf, a]
  #            (VERIFIED via Object.getOwnPropertyNames on the real result).
  #   * A key whose VALUE is undefined (but the key exists) IS copied:
  #       pick({a:undefined}, 'a')  ->  res.a = undefined  (res HAS own key 'a').
  #   * A key whose value is any falsy value (0, '', false, null) IS copied
  #     because existence — not truthiness — is the test:
  #       pick({a:0},'a')->{a:0}  pick({a:''},'a')->{a:''}
  #       pick({a:false},'a')->{a:false}  pick({a:null},'a')->{a:null}.
  #
  # OBSERVABLE-UNDER-JSON (the differential oracle serializes results with
  #   JSON.stringify; this is part of the contract the blind re-emitter must
  #   match, and it is satisfied AUTOMATICALLY by any faithful `res[k]=obj[k]`
  #   re-emitter):
  #   * A picked key whose value is `undefined` -> the key IS set on res, but
  #     JSON.stringify DROPS undefined-valued own keys, so the serialized
  #     result omits it. pick({a:undefined},'a') serializes to {} (yet res.a
  #     exists in memory). Real package VERIFIED: JSON => {}.
  #   * A picked key whose value is a FUNCTION (e.g. inherited 'toString',
  #     'constructor', 'hasOwnProperty', 'valueOf') -> the key IS set on res,
  #     but JSON.stringify DROPS function-valued own keys, so the serialized
  #     result omits it. pick({a:1},'toString') serializes to {} (yet
  #     res.toString is the inherited function). Real package VERIFIED: JSON => {}.
  #   Because BOTH the real package and any faithful re-emitter set the SAME
  #   property to the SAME value, their JSON serializations are byte-identical.
  #   No divergence arises from this — it is documented so the blind engineer
  #   does NOT "fix" it by switching to hasOwnProperty (which WOULD diverge:
  #   hasOwnProperty would also omit OWN undefined-valued keys from res, but
  #   the difference is invisible under JSON for undefined values, AND would
  #   visibly diverge for an OWN serializable inherited-name key — see the
  #   curated case pick({toString:'hi',a:1}, ['toString','a']) -> {toString:'hi',a:1},
  #   which the `in` operator AND hasOwnProperty BOTH pick, but which a
  #   truthiness or own-only-with-different-semantics impl could get wrong).

ORACLE  value -> oracle.json
  # Deterministic, pure. No clock / RNG / IO / network seam. No injection
  # needed. The oracle stores JSON.stringify(encode(args)) inputs and the
  # JSON-serialized return value.

SCOPE
  IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
    - The isObject guard predicate (B0/B1), inlined (isobject@3.0.1 verbatim).
    - The function-passes-guard exception (typeof obj === 'function').
    - The string-vs-array BRANCH (B3a/B3b) and the single-key early return.
    - The `in`-operator key-existence test (prototype-chain inclusive).
    - res = obj[key] copy semantics (existence, not truthiness; undefined and
      function values copied; vanish under JSON).
    - Fresh-object return on every path ({} on guard / empty / no-match).
  CARRIED DATA: none. (No literal tables; isobject is 1 inlined expression.)
  OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented capability boundaries; the
  oracle does NOT exercise these — they are non-round-trippable or non-string/
  non-array key types):
    - keys as a FUNCTION: object.pick@1.3.0 has NO predicate/filter handling
      (that exists only in LATER major versions). At 1.3.0, a function `keys`
      fails `typeof keys==='string'`, then `len = keys.length` is the function
      ARITY and `keys[idx]` are undefined indices, so the loop copies nothing:
      pick({a:1,b:2}, fn) -> {} for any function fn. VERIFIED. (Functions are
      not JSON-round-trippable, so excluded from genInputs.)
    - keys as null or undefined: THROWS TypeError "Cannot read properties of
      null/undefined (reading 'length')" — the array branch reads keys.length
      before any guard. VERIFIED. (undefined does not round-trip as a JSON
      value; excluded from genInputs.)
    - keys as a number / boolean: typeof not 'string', keys.length is undefined,
      ++idx < undefined is false -> loop never runs -> returns {}. VERIFIED.
      (Out-of-envelope: spec is string|string[]; excluded from genInputs.)
    - keys as a plain array-LIKE object {length:n, 0:..}: iterates like the
      array branch -> picks. VERIFIED. (Out-of-envelope key type; excluded.)
    - obj = function with own/inherited props: passes guard, props pickable
      (pick(fn_with_myprop,'myprop')->{myprop:7}). VERIFIED. (Functions not
      JSON-round-trippable; excluded from genInputs but documented in-contract
      for the guard.)
    - Symbol keys: typeof symbol !== 'string', and Symbols cannot be array
      members that JSON-round-trip; not exercised. The `in` operator would
      apply if passed in an array, but Symbols are out-of-envelope for the
      JSON oracle.
    - Property VALUES that are functions / undefined / symbols: copied by the
      logic but invisible under JSON serialization (see OBSERVABLE-UNDER-JSON);
      genInputs only emits JSON-round-trippable values so the stored expecteds
      are faithful.

PROVENANCE
